.\" OpenBSD-style concise man page
.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt ENV 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm env
.Nd run a command in a modified environment
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm env
.Op Fl i0
.Op Fl u Ar name
.Op Fl C Ar dir
.Op Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.Op Ar command Op Ar arg ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility runs
.Ar command
with a modified environment, or prints the current environment.
Each
.Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value
argument sets an environment variable before executing the command.
.Pp
If no
.Ar command
is specified,
.Nm
prints the resulting environment, one variable per line.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl i , Fl Fl ignore-environment
Start with an empty environment.
Without this option, the current environment is inherited.
.It Fl 0 , Fl z , Fl Fl null
End each output line with a NUL byte instead of a newline.
Only meaningful when printing the environment (no command).
.It Fl u Ar name , Fl Fl unset Ns = Ns Ar name
Remove
.Ar name
from the environment before running the command.
May be specified multiple times.
.It Fl C Ar dir , Fl Fl chdir Ns = Ns Ar dir
Change the working directory to
.Ar dir
before running the command or printing the environment.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
If no command is specified, the exit status is 0 on success
or 1 on failure.
.Pp
If a command is specified:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 125
An error occurred in
.Nm
itself (e.g., failed to change directory).
.It 126
The command was found but could not be invoked.
.It 127
The command could not be found.
.El
.Pp
Otherwise, the exit status is that of the executed command.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Print the current environment:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ env
.Ed
.Pp
Run a command with an additional variable:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ env LANG=C sort file.txt
.Ed
.Pp
Run a command with a clean environment:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ env -i PATH=/usr/bin HOME=/tmp sh
.Ed
.Pp
Remove a variable and run a command:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ env -u DISPLAY xterm
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr printenv 1 ,
.Xr sh 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility is compliant with the
.St -p1003.1-2008
specification.
The
.Fl C
and
.Fl 0
options are extensions compatible with GNU coreutils.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An "vibeutils implementation by Travis Cole"
